The **Multidimensional Poverty Index** (MPI), jointly released by the **UNDP** and **OPHI,** measures poverty by considering **deprivations beyond** the traditional **income-based approach**. It provides a **holistic view of** **poverty** in the following ways:
1. a) **Complements** traditional monetary poverty measures by capturing the **acute deprivations** in health, education, and living standards that a person faces simultaneously;
   b) estimates the **extent of** **poverty (**intensity) experienced at an **individual level** (through the percentage of deprivations).

2. a) Permits **comparisons** both across countries and world regions; b); its **disaggregated data** structure facilitates **detailed analysis** across ethnic group, urban/rural areas, subnational region, and age demographics;
   c) provides insight into key **household and community characteristics** (sanitation, electricity access).

3. a) Helps identify **patterns of poverty** (dimensional breakdown) enabling **targeted interventions** (Swachch Bharat Abhiyan);
   b) provides a **flexible framework** adaptable to **local contexts** (NITI Aayog’s National Multidimensional Poverty Index).

4. a) Highlights **interconnected deprivations** that can lead to **poverty traps** (indicators on undernourishment, years of schooling);
   b) acts as a critical tool to **mainstream SDG prioritization** (SDG 1,2,3,4 etc);
   c) encourages policy makers to **address systemic issues** rather than the **symptoms** **of poverty**.

Despite these advantages, some experts have argued that it **does not offer a comprehensive measure** of poverty, due to the following reasons:
1. a) **Uniform weightage** oversimplifies poverty by ignoring the varying significance of different deprivations;
   b) using **standardized** thresholds may not accurately reflect **regional diversity**.

2. a) Inconsistent** quality, and availability of **data** across regions and indicators can hinder the accurate measurement of multidimensional poverty;**
   b) may underemphasize the importance of **income growth** in poverty reduction.

3. a) **Overlooks** **key dimensions** which play an important role in determination of poverty (ownership of technology, environmental degradation);
   b) may conceal **intra-household differences** (gender, age, disability) by treating households uniformly.

Though, MPI’s broader framework more accurately reflects the complex realities of poverty. Incorporating **other** **metrics such as income fluctuation** adjustments and the status of **environmental degradation** would make it a more **comprehensive tool** for monitoring and achieving sustainable development. ***(487 words).***
